extends layout.pug

block content-block
  .page-content
    h1 Integrations
    p GNIS-LD is built to be queried by other systems, not just browsed. Every record is machine-readable, every identifier dereferences, and the SPARQL endpoint is open — so there are several ways to pull authoritative USGS place data into your own stack.

    h2 Federate with SPARQL
    p If you already run a SPARQL-capable store, you don't need to import anything. Add a #[code SERVICE] clause and your engine joins its own data with GNIS-LD at query time — results always reflect the current dataset:
    .code-card
      .code-card-head
        span.dot
        span.dot
        span.dot
        span.code-card-name federated.rq
      pre.
        <span class="kw">SELECT</span> ?official ?description <span class="kw">WHERE</span> {
          <span class="kw">SERVICE</span> <span class="str">&lt;https://#{base_url}/sparql&gt;</span> {
            ?peak gnis:alternativeName <span class="str">"Denali"@en</span> ;
                  gnis:officialName ?official ;
                  gnis:description ?description .
          }
        }
        <span class="cm"># "Mount McKinley" — "Elevation 20,310 ft.; the highest point in North America"</span>
    p This is the pattern to reach for when verifying facts against the federal record: keep your data where it is, and let the query bring the authoritative values in.

    h2 Query over HTTP
    p The endpoint speaks standard SPARQL over HTTP, so any HTTP client works. POST a query and choose your result format with the #[code Accept] header:
    .code-card
      pre.
        <span class="cm">$</span> curl -d <span class="str">'query=SELECT * WHERE { ?s ?p ?o } LIMIT 5'</span> \
            -H <span class="str">'Accept: application/sparql-results+json'</span> \
            https://#{base_url}/sparql
    p Prefer a friendlier starting point? The #[a(href='/explore') explore workspace] has runnable examples and visualizations, and the #[a(href='/search') search page] builds SPARQL for you.

    h2 Dereference identifiers
    p Every feature URI is a working URL. Request it with an RDF media type and you get the record itself, ready for your parser:
    .code-card
      pre.
        <span class="cm">$</span> curl -H <span class="str">'Accept: text/turtle'</span> \
            https://#{base_url}/lod/gnis/feature/1414314
    p This makes GNIS identifiers safe to store in your own database: they stay resolvable, citable, and self-describing.

    h2 Bulk download
    p Need everything locally? The complete database — roughly fourteen million triples — can be exported from the #[a(href='/data') data] endpoint and loaded into your own triple store.
